  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nCurrently, the trough axis extends across the forecast area with \nweak ridging focused over the Southeast US and the Desert Southwest. \nAt the surface, the frontal boundary that brought us cooler \ntemperatures and breezy northerly winds is drape across southern \nMissouri and into Oklahoma. High pressure is centered over the \nNorthern Plains and is building south. Dense low clouds and spotty \nshowers linger across much of the forecast area early this morning. \nThis activity will continue to clear out from the northwest as a \nmuch drier airmass filter in behind the front. Mostly clear \nconditions are expected for the majority of the forecast area by \nlunchtime. Winds will remain breezy out of the northeast, and \ntemperatures are only expected to warm into the upper 60s to lower \n70s areawide this afternoon. \n\nTemperatures are expected to remain below normal through Saturday as \nwe remain under the influence of the post frontal airmass. Ridging \nwill begin to try to build into the area this weekend, allowing \ntemperatures to rebound towards normal by Sunday. However, a weak \nshortwave will traverse the northern tier of the country, dragging a \nfrontal boundary across the central US late on Sunday. This will \nhelp to maintain near normal temperatures through Monday. Then as \nridging build in from the west by mid week, and especially later in \nthe week, temperatures will warm back above normal. Highs for \nTuesday and Wednesday are expected to reach into the lower 80s \nacross the region. Then, middle to upper 80s are expected by the end \nof the forecast period. \n\nNear zero precip chances are expected after this morning through the \nend of the forecast period. Upper level northwesterly flow and a \nmuch drier air mass will dominate through the forecast time\nframe.",
